course: dictionary
lesson: 3578
title: Few, a few and fewer
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Few means not many. «Few people came to the party.» Only a small number of people came, so the party was quiet. Use few with things you can count, like people, sweets or animals.
explain: «A few» is different. It means some, a small number. «I have a few sweets» means you have some sweets, not none. «A hedgehog curled up in a pile of leaves may breathe only a few times a minute» means only a small number of breaths each minute.
explain: Fewer compares two amounts, and fewest is the smallest of all. «There are far fewer predators than plant-eaters.» «She got the fewest mistakes in the class.» Ask yourself: is it a small number, or the smallest of all?
fact: Few means not many.
fact: «A few» means some, a small number.
fact: Fewer means a smaller number than another; fewest means the smallest number of all.
fact: In maths, «4 fewer than 11» means 11 − 4, which is 7.
fact: «Quite a few» means many, so always check the context.
exercise: «Few people came to the party.» What does few mean here? | Very loud | Quickly | *Not many | Lots of
exercise: «I have a few sweets.» What does «a few» mean? | Exactly one | None at all | Very many | *Some, a small number
exercise: Ben has 11 stickers. Sam has 4 fewer than Ben. How many stickers does Sam have? | *7 | 4 | 11 | 15
test: «She got the fewest mistakes in the class.» What does fewest mean? | Quite a lot | *The smallest number of all | The biggest number of all
test: Which word means a smaller number than another? | a few | *fewer | few | fewest
exam: Mia says «quite a few» means not many. Is she right? | *No, it means many | Yes, it means not many | It means none at all | It is a kind of sweet
